Distributed decomposition of the OCR XSBench lookup kernel.

The lookup range is cut into places (-p).  Each place owns a contiguous share
of the lookups, one producer over that share, and its own replica of the
physics table, and every object of a place is created at one policy domain
derived from the place index.  A lookup is still a three-task chain, so the
runtime work per lookup is what the per-lookup port does; what differs is that
no chain reaches across a policy domain and that production is as wide as the
number of places.

The table is generated in parallel by the tasks that own it, from a generator
that is a pure function of (stream, index), so replicas agree without
exchanging anything and the instance depends on neither the number of places
nor the number of nodes.  Verification is a checksum of the lookup results:
each lookup quantizes its macroscopic cross section to an integer weighted by
its lookup index, accumulated per place and summed once at the end, so the
value is invariant under partitioning and completion order.

Arguments
  -s <size>        H-M configuration (small, large, XL, XXL)
  -g <gridpoints>  gridpoints per nuclide
  -l <lookups>     total cross-section lookups
  -p <places>      partitions of the lookup range, one table replica each
  -b <batch>       lookup chains in flight per place; also the number of blocks
                   the unionized index plane is cut into

Offered concurrency is places x batch chains.  Memory is
places x n_isotopes x gridpoints x (4 x n_isotopes + 56) bytes.

To build and run

x86:
make -f Makefile.x86 clean run
